A||||4||overlord||coolant recov. tank question||||1||
Z||000000||overlord||04-27-2000||11:57 PM||||i noticed the other day that after i shut my car off i heard a hissing coming from up front...turns out the recovery bottle is cracked (someone has used some gunk of somekind to seal it) but didnt do it very well..there is air seaping out of the bottle.<P>anyways the question.<BR>will the cracked recovery tank affect the cooling system in any way?<P>my temp guage sometimes jumps up to redline before going back down to norm. if this has anything to do with it.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||04-28-2000||06:10 AM||||Quite sure the recovery tank is supposed to be vented. As long as your crack/hole is above the fluid level all should be ok. As for gage going to red sounds like fan coming on at a high temp-sucker doesn't come on until 235 F||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-28-2000||07:36 AM||||As your coolant heats up it expands.  Eventually some will spit out of the radiator cap via the hose to the coolant recovery tank.  As the engine cools the coolant contracts and sucks the coolant from the recovery tank back into the radiator.<P>If the tank is cracked and leaks, your coolant level in the engine will eventually diminish.  Also if the hose between the tank and the recovery tank leaks it will not suck the coolant back into the radiator upon cooling.<P>The tank is made out of a thermoplastic (either polypropylene or polyethylene).  This means that you can weld up a split seam or hole.  I had one split at the seam.  I scraped some excess material from the mold parting line on the tank and used it as filler material to weld the crack shut using a soldering gun.  It has held for almost two years so far.  Just heat up the crack enough to make it soft but not burn (you'll know it's too hot when it starts to turn black).  Then lay in some shavings and melt them into the crack.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||04-28-2000||02:46 PM||||I beleive that the dealer still sells the tank for something like $10. Real cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||04-28-2000||04:40 PM||||WK is right on, I picked up a new tank for $10.79 earlier this month from the local Pontiac dealer.  I haven't installed it yet.  Looks like two screws hold it on.  I'm waiting for the snow to clear.<P> 4/29/00<BR>I was wrong on the price, it was $8.20 from Mr Goodwrench.  Only 2 nuts to remove.  One you  haave to be a contortionist just to put a socket on it.  The sun came out today, time for a quick drive around and tank replacement.<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 04-29-2000).]||||reg||14
